PLAIN-SPEAK QUESTION

A49 showed us
that the core can control
the undecided cells around it.

The problem was that
most missing cells
were not undecided.

They were sitting on the bench.

A48 showed us
a safe way
to get some of those cells
off the bench sooner.

A50 puts those two
already-tested mechanisms together.

It does not make recovery stronger.

It does not make push-pull stronger.

It asks whether:

one layer
makes cells available,

while the other layer
organizes those available cells.

If the combined arm
does something neither layer
can do alone,

we will have direct evidence
for a layered developmental architecture:

recovery supplies plasticity;

the surviving core
supplies spatial organization;

and the macro behavior
comes from their interaction.

